---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const title = 'Mobile Shop Inventory Software — POS Plan';
const description =
  "Track stock automatically as you sell. POS Plan updates inventory on every sale and purchase, supports IMEI tracking for mobile shops, and works across multiple outlets.";
const canonical = 'https://erpboss.com/inventory';

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Does stock update automatically when I make a sale?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Yes. Every sale reduces stock for the items sold immediately, with no separate stock-adjustment step required.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Can I track individual phones by IMEI?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Yes. Products can be tracked by individual serial number or IMEI, so you know exactly which unit sold rather than just a total count.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Can I move stock between two of my outlets?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Yes, stock transfers between outlets are logged the same way a sale is, so nothing goes missing in a handoff between locations.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Base title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} jsonLd={[faqJsonLd]}>
  <Nav slot="nav" />

  <h1>Stock tracking that updates itself</h1>
  <p>
    The most common inventory mistake in a small shop isn't a bad process — it's no process.
    Stock gets counted by memory, and gaps only show up when a customer asks for something
    that's supposedly "in stock" but isn't. POS Plan closes that gap by tying stock directly to
    every sale and every purchase, automatically, so the number on screen matches what's on the
    shelf.
  </p>

  <h2>Stock moves with every transaction, not on a schedule</h2>
  <p>
    Sell an item and its quantity drops the moment the sale is completed. Buy stock in from a
    supplier and it's added the moment that purchase is recorded. A return brings it back. There
    is no nightly batch job or manual "sync stock" button — the count you see is the count as of
    right now.
  </p>

  <h2>Built for mobile shops: IMEI &amp; serial tracking</h2>
  <p>
    Selling phones means every unit is unique — two units of the "same model" are not
    interchangeable once one of them is sold. POS Plan lets you track individual items by IMEI
    or serial number, not just a generic product count, so you always know exactly which unit
    sold, and which specific units are still on the shelf. This matters for warranty claims and
    for repair jobs, where a device needs to be matched to its original sale.
  </p>

  <h2>Low-stock visibility</h2>
  <p>
    See what's running low before a customer asks for it and you have to say no. Instead of a
    manual stock-take or a spreadsheet export just to find out what to reorder, the dashboard
    surfaces low-stock items directly.
  </p>

  <h2>Purchases feed stock in, sales and returns take it back out</h2>
  <p>
    Recording a purchase from a supplier and completing a sale use the same stock ledger under
    the hood — so a report of "what's on hand" is always a real-time answer, not a snapshot from
    whenever someone last updated a spreadsheet. Returns adjust the same way, crediting stock
    back automatically.
  </p>

  <h2>Multiple outlets, one account</h2>
  <p>
    Running more than one shop? Stock is tracked per outlet, with transfers between outlets
    logged the same way a sale is — so nothing disappears in a handoff between locations, and
    each outlet's numbers stay accurate independently even as you also see them combined.
  </p>

  <p>See how billing and stock work together on the <a href="/barcode">barcode page</a>, how
  stock feeds into daily numbers on <a href="/reports">reports</a>, or check <a href="/pricing">pricing</a>.</p>
  <p><a href="https://app.erpboss.com/frontend/signup">Start your free trial</a></p>

  <Footer slot="footer" />
</Base>
